You’ve probably seen it floating around the internet…that some Italian teacher named Roberto Nevilis invented homework in 1905 to punish his students. That story is completely made up. He almost certainly never existed.

So where did homework actually come from?

In the 1840s an American educator named Horace Mann traveled to Prussia. Prussia had built a mandatory schooling system and it wasn’t designed around learning. It was designed around obedience. Compliance. National identity.

The Prussian government needed citizens who would follow orders, show up on time, and not question authority. Sound familiar?

Homework was a key part of that system. Not to reinforce learning but to make sure state-approved ideas followed children home. Into their families. Into their evenings. Into every corner of their lives.

Horace Mann was so impressed he brought the entire system back to America. And we’ve been using it ever since.

It actually became so controversial that in 1901 California banned homework for children under 15. Doctors were saying it was harmful to children’s health. But the ban didn’t last.

I spent 11 years teaching in this system. The more I learned about where it actually came from the clearer my decision became for my own boys.

This isn’t conspiracy. This is documented history. Save this and share it with a parent who needs to see it.
